067985307e-07,-5.745722592109814e-05,-1.2159273865108844e-05,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-5.054346183896996e-05,-5.960462772236497e-07,-1.311301275563892e-06,-1.0728830375228426e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,35,24308,279,897,315,279,23809,2629,198,59,26056,1242,15159,77,284,220,16,22,92,24884,258,36958,1124,37018,35702,6863,316,91362,15170,16,20,3417,35702,6863,316,91362,15170,16,22,3417,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nDetermine the value of the infinite sum\n\\[\\sum_{n = 17}^\\infty \\frac{\\binom{n}{15}}{\\binom{n}{17}}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"8a351685de939bec752303a7896d0d198f5d865df1fefe6f302a24f5da37c88e"},"seconds":21.92226266860962,"seed":824198304161708341,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the value of the infinite sum \\(\\sum_{n = 17}^\\infty \\frac{\\binom{n}{15}}{\\binom{n}{17}}\\), we first simplify the expression inside the sum.\n\nRecall the definition of the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\). Using this, we can express \\(\\binom{n}{15}\\) and \\(\\binom{n}{17}\\) as follows:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{15} = \\frac{n!}{15!(n-15)!} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\binom{n}{17} = \\frac{n!}{17!(n-17)!}.\n\\]\nThe ratio \\(\\frac{\\binom{n}{15}}{\\binom{n}{17}}\\) can be simplified as follows:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\binom{n}{15}}{\\binom{n}{17}} = \\frac{\\frac{n!}{15!(n-15)!}}{\\frac{n!}{17!(n-17)!}} = \\frac{17!(n-17)!}{15!(n-15)!} = \\frac{17 \\cdot 16}{(n-16)(n-17)} = \\frac{272}{(n-16)(n-17)}.\n\\]\nThus, the sum becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=17}^\\infty \\frac{\\binom{n}{15}}{\\binom{n}{17}} = \\sum_{n=17}^\\infty \\frac{272}{(n-16)(n-17)}.\n\\]\nWe can use partial fraction decomposition to simplify the term \\(\\frac{272}{(n-16)(n-17)}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{272}{(n-16)(n-17)} = \\frac{A}{n-16} + \\frac{B}{n-17}.\n\\]\nTo find \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), we solve the equation:\n\\[\n272 = A(n-17) + B(n-16).\n\\]\nSetting \\(n = 17\\) gives \\(272 = B\\), and setting \\(n = 16\\) gives \\(272 = A\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{272}{(n-16)(n-17)} = \\frac{272}{n-16} - \\frac{272}{n-17}.\n\\]\nThe sum now becomes:\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=17}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{272}{n-16} - \\frac{272}{n-17} \\right).\n\\]\nThis is a telescoping series. Writing out the first few terms, we get:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{272}{17-16} - \\frac{272}{17-17} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{272}{18-16} - \\frac{272}{18-17} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{272}{19-16} - \\frac{272}{19-17} \\right) + \\cdots\n\\]\n\\[\n= 272 - \\frac{272}{0} + \\frac{272}{2} - \\frac{272}{1} + \\frac{272}{3} - \\frac{272}{2} + \\cdots\n\\]\nAll the terms cancel out except for the first term \\(272\\). Therefore, the sum is:\n\\[\n272.\n\\]\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{272}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{272}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:799"}
